Análisis de la ruta de ataque

The original high severity is supported and retained. The vulnerability is in the main runtime API, is reachable through normal authenticated HTTP routes, has executable PoC evidence from validation, and crosses a central SaaS tenant boundary. The impact is significant because it enables unauthorized reads and writes/deletes of another tenant's media and promotional content, including content later used in outbound bot messaging. It is not raised to critical because it is not unauthenticated, requires the attacker to already be an admin in some tenant and at least a member of the victim tenant, and appears limited to media/promotions rather than full tenant data, platform control, identity compromise, or secret disclosure.

Ruta
Attacker with valid JWT: admin role in tenant A --admin role satisfies router dependency--> tenant_admin_router require_min_role('admin') checks unscoped request.state.roles --request reaches handler authz--> ensure_tenant_access accepts has_user_tenant_role for tenant B without role threshold --path tenant accepted--> Handler executes set_config('app.tenant_id', tenant B) --RLS context is set to victim tenant--> Tenant B media/promotions read/write/delete --active promotions are consumed by booking flow--> Promotion content can be queued into outbound bot messages
The finding is a real, in-scope authorization vulnerability. The JWT processing stores roles as a flat request.state.roles list and separately stores request.state.tenant_id. tenant_admin_router then accepts any admin role in that flat list. For media and promotion endpoints, the handler calls ensure_tenant_access, whose fallback query only checks whether the user has any user_tenant_roles row for the path tenant and does not require admin/owner there. Once that weak check passes, the handlers set app.tenant_id to the path tenant and perform tenant-scoped SQL reads and mutations. PostgreSQL RLS exists, but it is not an effective mitigation here because the application itself sets the RLS tenant context to the victim tenant after the weak any-membership check. The impact is cross-tenant modification of media assets and promotional content, including content that the booking flow can queue for outbound bot messages to the victim tenant's customers.

Probabilidad
High - Exploitation is straightforward once the attacker has the dual-tenant role condition: send normal authenticated HTTP requests with a different tenant_id path parameter. The required condition is plausible in a SaaS with tenant switching and shared users, but it is not unauthenticated and does require admin privilege somewhere plus membership in the victim tenant, so likelihood is medium rather than high. | Remote network vector
Impacto
High - This breaks tenant isolation for meaningful tenant-admin functionality. An attacker cannot act anonymously, but a user with admin in one tenant and low privilege in another can read media and promotions and perform create/update/delete operations in the other tenant. Promotion tampering can affect customer-facing bot messages, and media deletion/upload affects tenant content integrity and metadata confidentiality.
Suposiciones
Tenant admins are trusted only for their own tenant and not for tenants where they hold a lower role.
The JWT roles claim can contain an admin-equivalent role for one tenant while the same user has only a non-admin membership row for another tenant, as described by the tenant-switching and user_tenant_roles model.
The FastAPI API service is deployed as the main runtime product service; production ingress was not present in the checkout, so public exposure is inferred from the application and Docker configuration rather than cloud manifests.
Valid authenticated user JWT
JWT roles list contains admin or higher for some tenant or context
Attacker has any app.user_tenant_roles membership row in the target tenant, even if not admin/owner
Attacker knows or can select the target tenant_id path value; targeted update/delete also requires object IDs
Controles
JWT/OIDC authentication is required for tenant_admin_router
tenant_admin_router requires admin role, but the role check is unscoped
ensure_tenant_access checks tenant membership, but not the minimum role for the path tenant
PostgreSQL RLS is enabled on media_assets and promotions
Handlers set app.tenant_id to the path tenant before SQL access
Audit calls exist for successful create/update/delete operations but do not prevent unauthorized actions
Puntos ciegos
No production cloud ingress or load balancer manifests were present, so internet exposure is inferred from app/Docker configuration and the project threat model.
The exact production IdP role-claim format was not externally verified; assessment relies on checked-in JWT role handling and user_tenant_roles authorization logic.
Static review did not enumerate every tenant_admin_router endpoint for the same pattern beyond the reported media/promotions paths.
Object storage ACLs and signed URL behavior for stored media were not fully assessed; the core issue is authorization before media metadata/object operations.


